#use(std/code)
#use(std/toctree)

#section{Yet Another Mark Down Language}::

Indentation-based markup language compiling to an element IR (HTML backend first)

Inspiration, without any particular order:

#list(style: "-")
	#item: Typst, LaTeX
	#item: Ruby, Haml, ERB
	#item: Sphinx

Non-goals:

#list
	#item: Being HTML
	#item
		Being tied to Ruby or Racket. The host language is pluggable in principle,
		but a document is a program in exactly #emph{one} host (reference implementation: Racket)
	#item
		Being a build system, package manager or plugin ecosystem of its own.
		Plugins are ordinary host modules found through the host's tooling (Racket
		collections, #code{raco pkg}); YAMD adds no registry, lockfile or install
		step, and leans on the host's build tooling rather than growing its own

#section{Core Principles}
	#list
		#item
			#strong{One sigil.} #code{#} is the only globally special character. Every other symbol (#code{|}, #code{*},
			#code{$}, …) is plain text unless a surrounding function's reader gives it meaning
		#item
			#strong{One form per role.} Call, comment, escape — nothing else. Headings, lists, links, tables, math are all
			library functions, not syntax
		#item
			#strong{Documents are programs.} The sigil escapes from text mode into host mode; #code{{}} escapes back. Turing
			completeness comes from the host, not from a template mini-language
		#item: #strong{Two-pass evaluation} (collect → resolve) for cross-references, TOC, numbering, glossaries


#toctree(numbered: #t depth: 2)
	spec/index.yamd
	syntax/index.yamd
	stdlib/index.yamd
	cli/index.yamd

#section{Consciously Accepted Tradeoffs}
	#list
		#item: Documents are programs: no sandboxed rendering of untrusted documents; incremental/parallel builds are harder
		#item: One host at a time: a Ruby YAMD and a Racket YAMD would be different languages sharing a surface syntax
		#item
			#code{#} collides with hex colors, "issue ##42", shebangs in prose - mitigated by #code{##} and by code living in raw bodies
		#item: Indentation: tabs only, single tab per level
		#item: #code{args} grammar: full host expressions
		#item: Trailing #code{::}: no "until next same-name #code{::}" flat variant for now
		#item: Inline math: #code{#m{…}} only, no reader-macro shorthand
